data={'answer': 'Gladly does not provide an official, first-party Model Context Protocol (MCP) server [1][2]. Instead, the ecosystem relies on third-party integration platforms to bridge Gladly’s REST APIs with the MCP standard [3][4][5].

StackOne and Truto are the primary providers offering managed Gladly MCP servers [3][4]. These services dynamically generate MCP-compliant endpoints that translate Gladly’s API schemas into tools for AI agents, handling authentication and security automatically [3][4][5].

To connect an MCP client like Claude Desktop to a managed Gladly MCP server, you typically add the provider's generated URL to your configuration file [4][5]:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "gladly-integration": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": [
        "-y",
        "@modelcontextprotocol/server-sse",
        "--url",
        "https://api.provider-url.com/mcp/<your_secure_token>"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

While Gladly offers a robust App Platform for custom integrations and API access, building a custom MCP server requires manual maintenance of OAuth lifecycles and schema mapping [1][4][6][7].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://developer.gladly.com/rest/index.html', 'image': 'https://developer.gladly.com/rest/assets/permissions-agent.png', 'title': 'Gladly API', 'url': 'https://developer.gladly.com/rest/index.html'}, {'id': 'https://www.gladly.ai/gladly-developer-platform/', 'image': 'https://cdn.sanity.io/images/ny21dgz6/production/81520ffb6204cea7cfa08884e8f0494194a22383-1200x628.jpg?auto=format', 'title': 'Gladly developer platform: APIs, SDKs & integrations | Gladly', 'url': 'https://www.gladly.ai/gladly-developer-platform/'}, {'id': 'https://www.stackone.com/connectors/gladly/mcp/', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://www.stackone.com/connectors/gladly/mcp/'}, {'id': 'https://truto.one/blog/connect-gladly-to-chatgpt-manage-customers-tasks-and-support-data/', 'title': 'Connect Gladly to ChatGPT: Manage Customers, Tasks, and Support Data', 'url': 'https://truto.one/blog/connect-gladly-to-chatgpt-manage-customers-tasks-and-support-data/'}, {'id': 'https://truto.one/blog/connect-gladly-to-claude-sync-customer-profiles-and-inbox-workflows/', 'title': 'Connect Gladly to Claude: Sync Customer Profiles and Inbox Workflows', 'url': 'https://truto.one/blog/connect-gladly-to-claude-sync-customer-profiles-and-inbox-workflows/'}, {'id': 'https://help.gladly.com/developer-tutorials/docs/build-an-app-tutorial', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://help.gladly.com/developer-tutorials/docs/build-an-app-tutorial'}, {'id': 'https://help.gladly.com/developer-tutorials/docs/app-platform', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://help.gladly.com/developer-tutorials/docs/app-platform'}, {'id': 'https://www.stackone.com/connectors/gladly/', 'title': 'Gladly Integration for AI Agents', 'url': 'https://www.stackone.com/connectors/gladly/'}]} error=None log_id='log_G6-9exd95dZV'

data={'answer': 'The Slack MCP server is an official integration that allows AI agents to interact with Slack workspaces by searching channels, sending messages, managing canvases, and performing other Slack actions [1][2].

To develop with it, you can use the Slack-hosted MCP server or the open-source `@modelcontextprotocol/server-slack` package [1][3]. Slack supports JSON-RPC 2.0 over Streamable HTTP at the endpoint `https://mcp.slack.com/mcp` [1][4].

For local development, you can configure the server in your client (e.g., Claude Desktop) using `npx`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "slack": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-slack"],
      "env": {
        "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN": "xoxb-your-token",
        "SLACK_TEAM_ID": "T01234567"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

[3][5]', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slack-mcp-server.md', 'title': 'Slack MCP server overview', 'url': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slack-mcp-server.md'}, {'id': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/mcp-overview', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/mcp-overview'}, {'id': 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@modelcontextprotocol/server-slack', 'title': '@modelcontextprotocol/server-slack', 'url': 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@modelcontextprotocol/server-slack'}, {'id': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slack-mcp-server/developing.md', 'title': 'Developing a sample app with the Slack MCP Server', 'url': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slack-mcp-server/developing.md'}, {'id': 'https://mcpserver.dev/s/slack', 'title': 'Slack MCP Server - Setup, Features & Integration Guide', 'url': 'https://mcpserver.dev/s/slack'}, {'id': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slackbot-mcp-client', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.slack.dev/ai/slackbot-mcp-client'}, {'author': 'Slack', 'id': 'https://slack.com/help/articles/48855576908307-Guide-to-Model-Context-Protocol-in-Slack', 'image': 'https://a.slack-edge.com/80588/marketing/img/meta/slack_hash_256.png', 'title': 'Guide to Model Context Protocol in Slack | Slack', 'url': 'https://slack.com/help/articles/48855576908307-Guide-to-Model-Context-Protocol-in-Slack'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/zencoderai/slack-mcp-server', 'title': 'zencoderai/slack-mcp-server', 'url': 'https://github.com/zencoderai/slack-mcp-server'}]} error=None log_id='log_MpEMblFaBm36'

data={'answer': 'The Pumble Model Context Protocol (MCP) server is an official integration that allows third-party AI agents, such as Claude, Gemini, and Codex, to access your Pumble workspace to search messages, retrieve information, and perform actions [1][2][3].

To set it up, create a custom app in your Pumble workspace settings to generate an App key, user token, and bot token [4][3]. You then configure your local AI client (e.g., Claude CLI) by adding the Pumble MCP server endpoint to your configuration file [1][2].

Example configuration for Claude CLI in `.mcp.json`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pumble-mcp": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.pumble.com/mcp",
      "headers": {
        "token": "user/bot token",
        "x-app-token": "xpat-token"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The server supports various tools, including message searching, channel management, and scheduling, depending on the scopes granted during app creation [5][2].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://pumble.com/help/integrations/automation-workflow-integrations/how-to-use-the-pumble-mcp-server/', 'image': 'https://pumble.com/help/wp-content/themes/pumble-learn/assets/images/pumble-logo-cake.svg', 'publishedDate': '2026-06-02T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': 'How to use the Pumble MCP server - Pumble Help', 'url': 'https://pumble.com/help/integrations/automation-workflow-integrations/how-to-use-the-pumble-mcp-server/'}, {'author': 'Ana Erkic Lazendic', 'id': 'https://pumble.com/learn/pumble/ai-automation-guide/', 'image': 'https://pumble.com/learn/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/How_to_Use_AI_and_Automation_in_Pumble__API__AI_Assistant__and_MCP_Server.webp', 'publishedDate': '2026-08-19T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': 'How to Use AI & Automation in Pumble (API, MCP, AI Assistant)', 'url': 'https://pumble.com/learn/pumble/ai-automation-guide/'}, {'id': 'https://cake.com/updates/mcp-server', 'image': 'https://cake.com/assets/img/suite.png?v=20251128', 'title': 'MCP Server | Pumble | CAKE.com Updates', 'url': 'https://cake.com/updates/mcp-server'}, {'id': 'https://pumble.com/mcp', 'image': 'https://pumble.com/assets/images/redesign/og-images/ai-assistant.png', 'publishedDate': '2017-09-01T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': 'Connect AI agents with the Pumble MCP', 'url': 'https://pumble.com/mcp'}, {'id': 'https://dev-docs.marketplace.cake.com/pumble/build/mcp-server.html', 'title': 'How to use the Pumble MCP server - CAKE.com Marketplace Documentation', 'url': 'https://dev-docs.marketplace.cake.com/pumble/build/mcp-server.html'}, {'author': 'Martina Stojkovski', 'id': 'https://pumble.com/learn/pumble/connecting-pumble-with-ai-agents-mcp/', 'image': 'https://pumble.com/learn/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Connecting-Pumble-to-your-fav-AI-agents-social.webp', 'publishedDate': '2026-07-10T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': '