data={'answer': 'There is no single official Binance-developed Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. All available Binance MCP servers are community-developed projects.

Several independent implementations exist, such as the TypeScript-based server by nirholas [1][2] and the Python-based amazing-binance-mcp [3]. These servers allow AI clients like Claude Desktop or Cursor to interact with Binance APIs for trading, market data, and account management [1][4][5].

To use one, you must configure your MCP client with your Binance API credentials [4][3][6]. For example, to use the nirholas implementation in Claude Desktop:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "binance": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@nirholas/binance-mcp"],
      "env": {
        "BINANCE_API_KEY": "your-api-key",
        "BINANCE_SECRET_KEY": "your-secret-key"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Always verify the source and permissions of any third-party MCP server before providing your API keys [3].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://github.com/nirholas/Binance-MCP', 'title': 'nirholas/Binance-MCP', 'url': 'https://github.com/nirholas/Binance-MCP'}, {'id': 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@nirholas/binance-mcp-server', 'title': '@nirholas/binance-mcp-server', 'url': 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@nirholas/binance-mcp-server'}, {'id': 'https://pypi.org/project/amazing-binance-mcp/', 'title': 'amazing-binance-mcp v0.1.0', 'url': 'https://pypi.org/project/amazing-binance-mcp/'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/nirholas/bnb-chain-toolkit/blob/main/docs/mcp-servers.md', 'title': 'docs/mcp-servers.md', 'url': 'https://github.com/nirholas/bnb-chain-toolkit/blob/main/docs/mcp-servers.md'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/AnalyticAce/BinanceMCPServer', 'title': 'AnalyticAce/binance-mcp-server', 'url': 'https://github.com/AnalyticAce/BinanceMCPServer'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/ethancod1ng/binance-mcp-server/blob/main/README.md', 'title': 'README.md at main · ethancod1ng/binance-mcp-server', 'url': 'https://github.com/ethancod1ng/binance-mcp-server/blob/main/README.md'}, {'id': 'https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/iuk-ink/binance-mcp-server', 'image': 'https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/iuk-ink/binance-mcp-server/og-image', 'title': 'Binance MCP Server by iuk-ink | Glama', 'url': 'https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/iuk-ink/binance-mcp-server'}, {'favicon': 'https://pypi.org/static/images/favicon.35549fe8.ico', 'id': 'https://pypi.org/project/binance-mcp-server/1.2.4/', 'image': 'https://pypi.org/static/images/twitter.abaf4b19.webp', 'title': 'binance-mcp-server 1.2.4', 'url': 'https://pypi.org/project/binance-mcp-server/1.2.4/'}]} error=None log_id='log_Onaz_FqYPI_8'

data={'answer': 'Paygent Connect is a payment processing module provided by Paygent for integrating credit card and convenience store payments into e-commerce platforms [1][2].

To access the API, you must contract with Paygent and undergo a screening process [1][3]. Upon approval, you can request the connection kit, which includes the necessary PHP modules, client certificates, and CA files [4][5]. Pricing and specific contract terms are handled directly through Paygent's sales or support channels [1][3].

Configuration requires setting your merchant ID, connection ID, and password within the module's properties file or your platform's admin interface [4][1][2].

```php
// Example initialization
include_once("vendor/autoload.php");
use PaygentModule\System\PaygentB2BModule;
$p = new PaygentB2BModule();
```

