data={'answer': 'Reducto provides an official Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that allows AI agents to perform document tasks like parsing, extracting, splitting, classifying, and editing without custom integration code [1][2][3].

You can run the server in two ways:

Hosted: Use `https://mcp.reducto.ai/mcp` with an `Authorization: Bearer <your-api-key>` header. This is best for quick demos and web-based workflows but only supports public URLs [1][3].

Local: Run `uvx mcp-server-reducto` (requires Python 3.11+). This supports local file paths and integrates with your existing Reducto CLI credentials [1][3].

To add it to your client, use the following configuration for local execution:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "reducto": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["mcp-server-reducto"]
    }
  }
}
```

For Claude Code, you can also use the command `claude mcp add -s user reducto -- uvx mcp-server-reducto` [4][3].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://docs.reducto.ai/mcp-server', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.reducto.ai/mcp-server'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/reductoai/mcp-server-reducto', 'title': 'reductoai/mcp-server-reducto', 'url': 'https://github.com/reductoai/mcp-server-reducto'}, {'id': 'https://reducto.ai/SKILL.md', 'title': 'Reducto', 'url': 'https://reducto.ai/SKILL.md'}, {'id': 'https://reducto.mintlify.app/quickstart', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://reducto.mintlify.app/quickstart'}, {'id': 'https://app.reducto.ai/', 'title': 'Reducto', 'url': 'https://app.reducto.ai/'}, {'id': 'https://pypi.org/project/mcp-server-reducto/', 'title': 'mcp-server-reducto v0.3.0', 'url': 'https://pypi.org/project/mcp-server-reducto/'}, {'id': 'https://docs.reducto.ai/overview', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.reducto.ai/overview'}, {'id': 'https://reducto.mintlify.app/agent-guide', 'title': 'Reducto API Reference for Coding Agents', 'url': 'https://reducto.mintlify.app/agent-guide'}]} error=None log_id='log_EHb5W0y36Hi3'

data={'answer': 'The official Devin MCP server is a hosted service that allows MCP-compatible AI agents and IDEs to programmatically access Devin’s platform capabilities, including session management, playbooks, and repository documentation [1][2][3]. It is not installed locally; you connect your client to the remote endpoint at `https://mcp.devin.ai/mcp` [1][3].

To use it, generate a service user API key (prefixed with `cog_`) from your Devin account settings [1][2]. Configure your client using the following JSON structure:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "devin": {
      "serverUrl": "https://mcp.devin.ai/mcp",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer <API_KEY>"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

If you are using an enterprise account, you must also include your organization ID in the headers: `"X-Org-Id": "<YOUR_ORG_ID>"` [1][2]. Legacy API keys (`apk_` prefix) are not supported [1][3].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://mcp.devin.ai/', 'title': 'Devin MCP Server', 'url': 'https://mcp.devin.ai/'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devin.ai/work-with-devin/devin-mcp', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.devin.ai/work-with-devin/devin-mcp'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devinenterprise.com/work-with-devin/devin-mcp', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.devinenterprise.com/work-with-devin/devin-mcp'}, {'id': 'https://fast.io/resources/devin-ai-mcp-server/', 'image': 'https://fast.io/_astro/devin-ai-mcp-server-hero.wP1vtEIX.png', 'publishedDate': '2026-08-05T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': 'Configure and Use the Devin AI MCP Server: A Complete Guide | Fastio', 'url': 'https://fast.io/resources/devin-ai-mcp-server/'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devin.ai/cli/extensibility/mcp/configuration', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.devin.ai/cli/extensibility/mcp/configuration'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devin.ai/cli/extensibility/mcp/overview', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.devin.ai/cli/extensibility/mcp/overview'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devinenterprise.com/work-with-devin/mcp', 'title': 'mcp', 'url': 'https://docs.devinenterprise.com/work-with-devin/mcp'}, {'id': 'https://docs.devin.ai/desktop/cascade/mcp', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://docs.devin.ai/desktop/cascade/mcp'}]} error=None log_id='log_t_eo8BI2Z2vp'

data={'answer': 'Mermaid CLI is an open-source AI coding assistant for the terminal that supports multiple model providers [1]. It does not have a centralized API pricing or approval partner program; it is a local-first tool that uses your own API keys for remote providers or local inference via Ollama [1][2].

For technical access, the optional `mermaidd` service exposes a local Unix-socket or TCP control surface [2]. Mutating commands require a pairing token, which you can generate using `mermaid pair --label <device>` [2]. You then provide this token via the `auth.token` field or the `MERMAID_DAEMON_TOKEN` environment variable [2][3].

```rust
// Example: Creating a client with a pairing token
let client = RuntimeClient::daemon_with_token("your_token_here");
client.approve("approval_id_here")?;
```

The CLI handles provider authentication via environment variables or the OS keyring (e.g., `mermaid login <provider>`) [1][2].', 'citations': [{'id': 'https://docs.rs/crate/mermaid-cli/latest/source/README.md', 'title': 'mermaid-cli 0.25.0 - Docs.rs', 'url': 'https://docs.rs/crate/mermaid-cli/latest/source/README.md'}, {'id': 'https://crates.io/crates/mermaid-cli', 'title': 'mermaid-cli', 'url': 'https://crates.io/crates/mermaid-cli'}, {'id': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/mermaid_cli/runtime_client/protocol/enum.DaemonRequest.html', 'title': 'DaemonRequest in mermaid_cli::runtime_client::protocol - Rust', 'url': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/mermaid_cli/runtime_client/protocol/enum.DaemonRequest.html'}, {'id': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/src/mermaid_cli/runtime_client/client.rs.html', 'title': 'client.rs - source', 'url': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/src/mermaid_cli/runtime_client/client.rs.html'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid-cli?tab=readme-ov-file', 'publishedDate': '2020-05-18T00:00:00.000Z', 'title': 'mermaid-js/mermaid-cli', 'url': 'https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid-cli?tab=readme-ov-file'}, {'id': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/mermaid_cli/providers/discovery/index.html', 'title': 'mermaid_cli::providers::discovery - Rust', 'url': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/mermaid_cli/providers/discovery/index.html'}, {'id': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/src/mermaid_cli/cli/args.rs.html', 'title': 'args.rs - source', 'url': 'https://docs.rs/mermaid-cli/latest/src/mermaid_cli/cli/args.rs.html'}, {'id': 'https://github.com/noahsabaj/mermaid-cli/blob/714b2e734997d75fa331930284071ac27deee927/docs/adding_providers.md', 'title': 'docs/adding_providers.md', 'url': 'https://github.com/noahsabaj/mermaid-cli/blob/714b2e734997d75fa331930284071ac27deee927/docs/adding_providers.md'}]} error=None log_id='log_SoDPZdVz5oRl'

data={'answer': 'There is no single official YouTube Transcript MCP server provided by the Model Context Protocol developers. Instead, the community has developed several independent, open-source MCP servers for this purpose.

Popular implementations include:

- jkawamoto/mcp-youtube-transcript [1][2]
- sinco-lab/mcp-youtube-transcript [3]
- toorop/youtube-transcript-mcp [4]
- suckerfish/yttranscript_mcp [5]
- rklcheung/youtube-transcript-mcp (a remote-hosted option) [6]

These servers generally expose a get_transcript tool. To use one, add its configuration to your claude_desktop_config.json file. For example, using the sinco-lab version:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "youtube-transcript": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@sinco-lab/mcp-youtube-transcript"]
    }
  }
}
```

